# An exception no clause matches leaves the try and stops the program instead
# of being silently dropped (uncaught_exception.exit records the status). The
# oracle is the reference: an unhandled error is fatal, not forgotten.
#
# Nothing is printed before the failing statement on purpose — the oracle
# collects output and loses it when a program dies (see plan.md), so a case
# that printed first could not compare equal on stdout across the engines.
# If the exception were cleared instead of propagated, "not reached" would
# print and the status would be 0, failing this case twice over.

xs = [1, 2, 3]
try:
    v = xs[9]
except TypeError:
    print("wrong clause")
print("not reached")
